The open profile gains most (-6.6..-6.8 %): there every line end was an exception of the DNA pack. Decode costs +3..+20 %
on the CPU (the sink copies each block once more, in cache); the two-pass fallback (AX_LIT_TILE=0) costs chr1 0.094 s.
Encode +40-50 % (the split copies the input once; single-threaded memchr - not optimised).

## The GPU exceptions kernel (open profile: every byte that is not ACGT/acgt is an exception: position gap + value)
corpus  open archive: exceptions          gap+val pieces   with AX_LINEMODEL: exceptions   gap+val
chr1    4 611 853 (1286 per 64 KiB chunk)  1 206 968 B      3 135 (0.9 per chunk; IUPAC)     2 332 B
T2T     38 616 137 (815 per chunk)         16 403 877 B     0                                0 B
GRCh38  60 979 068 (1287 per chunk)        15 988 470 B     31 160 (0.7 per chunk)           34 728 B
(N runs are matches, not exceptions: N in GRCh38 160 M bytes, 31 160 exceptions left.) On the GPU the exception work
(the gap / val rANS pieces, the scatter of AX_OPEN_EXC in the bases store) goes to ~0; instead the line ends have to be
put back: either in the store of the block output (position o = record start + x + x / W per byte run: one division
per 16-byte store, no extra pass) or one expansion pass over the output (T2T 3.16 GB read + write: ~2 ms at ~3 TB/s on
an H100 against ~20 ms for the whole decode). Estimate, not measured: the T-H runs carry no AX_LINEMODEL archive yet.
